================================================================================
1. What FanForge actually contains
================================================================================

FanForge contains no third-party source code. Every source file under `src/` and
`tests/` was written for this project.

It links against nothing but the C++ standard library and, on Windows, the
operating system. It ships no bundled driver: the port-I/O path loads InpOut32 or
WinRing0 at runtime *if the user has installed one*, and the preferred path uses
the `\\.\APPLESMC` device that Apple's own Boot Camp support software publishes.
Neither is distributed here.

================================================================================
2. Protocol facts, and why they are not code
================================================================================

The SMC's register interface — the I/O port pair, the status bits, the command
bytes, the key names, the type encodings — is an interface to hardware that
already exists. The values of that interface were reverse-engineered by others
and documented publicly. Those values are facts, and facts are not copyrightable
expression; the same ones appear independently in several licences.

FanForge was written from the following public documentation of that interface.
Facts and design lessons were taken, not code:

  * Linux kernel, drivers/hwmon/applesmc.c
      Copyright (C) 2007 Nicolas Boichat, (C) 2010 Henrik Rydberg
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only
      Used for: the port protocol. Port pair 0x300/0x304; status bits
      SMC_STATUS_AWAITING_DATA (0x01), SMC_STATUS_IB_CLOSED (0x02),
      SMC_STATUS_BUSY (0x04); command bytes READ (0x10), WRITE (0x11),
      GET_KEY_BY_INDEX (0x12), GET_KEY_TYPE (0x13); the four-byte key argument
      followed by a length byte; the trailing flush loop; and the ten-fan cap.

  * Linux kernel, Apple T2 SMC driver (as cited in RPMAC's source)
      Used for: the observation that `flt` values are native little-endian IEEE-754
      by both the port and MMIO paths, while the fixed-point families are
      big-endian.

  * Apple's `AppleSMC` interface, as documented in the Darwin sources
      Used for: the `\\.\APPLESMC` IOCTL contract and the key enumeration model.

  * charlie754/mac-fan-control-windows (MacFanCtl)
      Licence: MIT
      Used for: the IOCTL codes and request struct layouts (read request = key +
      size; write request = key + size + payload; the six-byte key-info response;
      the big-endian key-by-index argument), verified by its author on a
      MacBookPro14,3, and the report that the device path also works on a T2
      MacBookPro15,1.

  * golirt1/RPMAC
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only
      Used for: the cross-process SMC mutex name `Global\AppleSmcAccess`
      (published by that project explicitly so other tools can adopt it), the
      per-fan `F<n>Md` manual-control path on T2 machines, and the fan-sensor
      plausibility bounds (`mn` may be 0 because some fans can stop; `mx` and `ac`
      up to 20000).

  * bmats/fancontrol, and the smcFanControl / HWSensors lineage via publication
      Used for: how manual fan control is expressed on these machines — the `FS!`
      force mask and the `F<n>Tg` target key.

================================================================================
3. What was deliberately not done
================================================================================

Macs Fan Control (CrystalIDEA) is closed-source, and was not decompiled, not
disassembled and not otherwise reverse-engineered. It is a *product* reference for
which features a fan control application should have. Its source was never
consulted, and none of its code or data appears here.

This was a deliberate choice and not only a legal one: the sources listed above
are better. Their protocol observations come attached to the specific hardware
they were verified on, and they are readable, which the binary is not.

================================================================================
4. Licence position
================================================================================

Two of the sources above are GPL-2.0. Because only interface facts were taken from
them, and because every line of FanForge is independently written in a different
language and a different architecture from the reference works, FanForge is not a
derivative work of them and is distributed under the MIT licence (see LICENSE).

The same interface facts appear independently in the MIT-licensed MacFanCtl,
which is an additional reason to be confident that they are interface, not
expression.

If you believe something here is mistaken, please open an issue rather than
guessing at intent: the goal is to be correct about this, and it is easy to fix.
